.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt CLOUT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clout
.Nd colorize command standard output and standard error
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl e Ar color
.Op Fl o Ar color
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
executes
.Ar command
and independently colorizes its standard output and standard error streams.
.Pp
The standard output and standard error streams of
.Ar command
are captured through separate pipes and written to the corresponding output streams using ANSI color escape sequences.
.Pp
The default color for standard output is green.
The default color for standard error output is red.
.Pp
.Nm
uses
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr execvp 3
to execute the command, and
.Xr poll 2
to monitor both output streams.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h
Display usage information and exit.
.It Fl e Ar color
Set the color used for standard error output.
.It Fl o Ar color
Set the color used for standard output.
.El
.Sh COLORS
The following colors are supported:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm black
Black.
.It Cm red
Red.
.It Cm green
Green.
.It Cm yellow
Yellow.
.It Cm blue
Blue.
.It Cm purple
Purple.
.It Cm cyan
Cyan.
.It Cm white
White.
.It Cm gray
Gray.
.It Cm bright-red
Bright red.
.It Cm bright-green
Bright green.
.It Cm bright-yellow
Bright yellow.
.It Cm bright-blue
Bright blue.
.It Cm bright-purple
Bright purple.
.It Cm bright-cyan
Bright cyan.
.It Cm bright-white
Bright white.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run
.Xr ls 1
using the default colors:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clout ls /
.Ed
.Pp
Color standard output green and standard error red explicitly:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clout -o green -e red ls /
.Ed
.Pp
Use cyan for standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clout -o cyan printf 'hello\en'
.Ed
.Pp
Use yellow for standard error output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clout -e yellow sh -c 'echo error >&2'
.Ed
.Pp
Display usage information:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clout -h
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
If the command terminates normally,
.Nm
exits with the command's exit status.
If the command terminates due to a signal,
.Nm
exits with a status of 128 plus the signal number.
.Pp
If
.Nm
fails to execute the command or encounters a fatal error, it exits with a non-zero status.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execvp 3 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr dup2 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
